# 17. DATABASE & TECHNICAL ARCHITECTURE

Build on the existing technology stack.

Do not introduce a new framework or database without a strong technical reason.

### Architecture Requirements

Use a shared multi-brand content model.

Suggested entities:

1. Brands
2. ContentItems
3. Campaigns
4. TeamMembers
5. ActivityLogs

### Suggested Relationships

- One Brand → Many Content Items.
- One Brand → Many Campaigns.
- One Campaign → Many Content Items.
- One Team Member → Many Assigned Content Items.
- One Content Item → Many Activity Log Entries.

### Data Integrity

- Use unique IDs.
- Maintain relationships using stable identifiers.
- Validate all incoming data.
- Prevent duplicate records where appropriate.
- Preserve existing records.
- Use safe migrations.
- Add appropriate database indexes.
- Handle errors gracefully.

### Persistence

All created and edited content must persist after refreshing the application.

Use the existing database if available.

If no persistent backend exists, choose a suitable persistent storage solution supported by the current environment.

Do not rely on temporary React state as the only data source.

Do not use localStorage as the only production data persistence layer.

### Security

- Validate inputs.
- Sanitize user-provided content.
- Protect sensitive operations.
- Respect existing authentication and permissions.
- Never expose API keys in frontend code.
- Keep secrets in environment variables.
- Apply appropriate database access controls.

